Flight Optimization Research Project

This demo now supports real airway data instead of only the toy waypoint graph.
It also lets you choose a single workflow instead of running every algorithm every time.

What is included:

- Airway network loading with `networkx`
- Wind field model for headwind and tailwind effects
- Aircraft mass-dependent fuel burn
- PSO route optimization with `pyswarms`
- Optuna hyperparameter tuning
- DEAP NSGA-II multi-objective optimization
- Monte Carlo weather uncertainty
- Route visualization
- Pareto front visualization

Real data sources:

- OpenSky trajectory tables, converted into a corridor graph by snapping observed points
- EUROCONTROL EAD / AIXM XML exports, or normalized node and edge tables
- FAA CIFP and NATS-style route tables, using the same normalized node and edge schema
- A synthetic fallback graph when no data path is supplied

New runtime entry points:

- `airway_data.py` normalizes source data into one graph format
- `network_model.py` exposes `GRAPH`, `NODES`, `AIRWAYS`, `ROUTE_CANDIDATES`
- `route_model.py` maps PSO particles onto k-shortest candidate routes
- `main-pso-hyp-mcarlo.py` accepts source, path, origin, destination, and workflow arguments

Workflow modes:

- `pso`: run one PSO search with fixed hyperparameters
- `optuna`: tune the PSO hyperparameters, then rerun PSO with the best trial
- `monte-carlo`: evaluate a candidate route under repeated weather uncertainty
- `pareto`: run the NSGA-II Pareto front search

How to run:

```bash
python main-pso-hyp-mcarlo.py --mode pso --airway-source synthetic --aircraft A320
python main-pso-hyp-mcarlo.py --mode optuna --airway-source synthetic --aircraft A320
python main-pso-hyp-mcarlo.py --mode monte-carlo --airway-source synthetic --route-index 0
python main-pso-hyp-mcarlo.py --mode pareto --airway-source synthetic
python main-pso-hyp-mcarlo.py --airway-source synthetic --aircraft A320
python main-pso-hyp-mcarlo.py --airway-source opensky --airway-path examples/opensky_corridor_sample.csv --origin KJFK --destination KLAX
python main-pso-hyp-mcarlo.py --airway-source eurocontrol --airway-path data/ead_export.xml --origin LFPG --destination EGLL
python main-pso-hyp-mcarlo.py --airway-source faa --airway-path data/faa_cifp_extract --origin KJFK --destination KLAX
python main-pso-hyp-mcarlo.py --airway-source nats --airway-path data/nats_tracks.csv
```

Accepted input shapes:

- Normalized route tables:
  - `nodes.csv` or `waypoints.csv` with `node_id`, `lat`, `lon`, and optional `kind` or `name`
  - `edges.csv` or `airways.csv` with `from`, `to`, and optional `distance_km` or `airway`
- OpenSky or NATS track tables:
  - Latitude and longitude columns such as `lat` and `lon`
  - Optional time, flight, callsign, or aircraft identifiers
- EUROCONTROL XML:
  - AIXM-style `DesignatedPoint`, `Navaid`, `AirportHeliport`, `Route`, and `RouteSegment` exports

Notes:

- The real-data loaders fall back to the synthetic demo network if the provided path is missing or cannot be parsed.
- Track data is snapped to a small spatial grid before route candidates are extracted.
- Candidate routes are built with k-shortest-path search over the loaded graph, so the optimizer searches among actual route alternatives rather than hardcoded branches.
- NSGA-II now uses mean fuel, mean time, and weather-risk variability, so the Pareto front is a real tradeoff set instead of a single deterministic optimum.
